  <!-- ============ HOURLY VS FLAT ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <h2 class="section__title">Why We Quote Flat and Never Hourly</h2>
        <p>
          When a cleaning company bills hourly, the incentive structure quietly inverts. The slower the
          work, the larger the invoice — and you have no independent way to know whether four hours was
          reasonable or whether two and a half would have done. Even with entirely well-intentioned
          cleaners, hourly billing transfers the risk of an under-estimated job from the company to you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Flat-rate quoting reverses that. We walk the property, count bedrooms and bathrooms, note the
          condition of the things that take time — grout, grease, pet hair, hard-water scale — and then
          commit to a number. If the job takes longer than we anticipated, that is our problem to absorb,
          not an extra line on your invoice. It is a better deal for the client, and it forces us to
          actually understand the work rather than guessing and billing for the error.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical consequence is that our quotes are sometimes higher than a competitor's hourly
          estimate up front, and almost always lower than the final invoice that estimate turns into.
          When you compare quotes, ask specifically whether the number is flat or hourly, and what is
          excluded. That single question explains most of the difference between two proposals.
        </p>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">Discounts and Credits</h2>
        <ul class="tick-list">
          <li><%- icon('checkCircle', 18) %> <span><strong>Referral credit:</strong> $40 for you and $40 for the neighbour you send us, unlimited.</span></li>
          <li><%- icon('checkCircle', 18) %> <span><strong>Seniors &amp; veterans:</strong> 10% off every visit, automatically applied.</span></li>
          <li><%- icon('checkCircle', 18) %> <span><strong>Recurring plans:</strong> all recurring rates are already discounted against one-time pricing.</span></li>
          <li><%- icon('checkCircle', 18) %> <span><strong>Portfolio pricing:</strong> landlords and property managers with three or more units get volume rates.</span></li>
          <li><%- icon('checkCircle', 18) %> <span><strong>Multi-site commercial:</strong> consolidated invoicing and rates that improve with volume.</span></li>
          <li><%- icon('checkCircle', 18) %> <span><strong>Deep-clean-first bundle:</strong> book a deep clean then a recurring plan and we discount the first recurring visit.</span></li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">Payment &amp; Invoicing</h2>
        <p>
          We accept all major credit and debit cards, ACH bank transfer and Apple Pay. Residential clients
          are charged on the day of service. Commercial clients receive consolidated monthly invoicing with
          a per-location breakdown, and construction projects can be invoiced per draw so cleaning costs map
          onto your project accounting. No deposit is required for standard residential work; contracts over
          $2,000 carry a 25% mobilisation deposit credited against the first invoice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PRICING IN DETAIL ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <h2 class="section__title">How to Compare Cleaning Quotes Properly</h2>
        <p>
          The hardest part of hiring a cleaner is not finding a price, it is finding out what the price
          covers. Two quotes for a three-bedroom house can differ by two hundred dollars and both look
          reasonable in isolation, because one of them silently excludes the oven, the interior windows,
          the baseboards and the grout. Comparing headline numbers without comparing scope is the most
          expensive mistake clients make, and it is entirely avoidable.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ask every company you are considering for the same five things in writing. If they cannot supply
          them, that is your answer.
        </p>
        <ul class="tick-list">
          <li><strong>The scope, room by room.</strong> Not "full house clean" but which surfaces, which appliances, whether inside the oven and refrigerator are included, whether baseboards and door frames are wiped, and whether interior windows are in or out.</li>
          <li><strong>The crew size and estimated duration.</strong> This tells you what the job genuinely takes. A low price against a two-person, five-hour scope is not a bargain, it is a shortcut you will find later.</li>
          <li><strong>What is excluded and why.</strong> Legitimate exclusions exist — mould remediation, biohazard, exterior windows above ground level, anything requiring a ladder over two steps — and a good company names them up front.</li>
          <li><strong>Whether supplies and equipment are included.</strong> Some operators quote labour only and expect you to provide products, a vacuum and cloths. Ours are always included.</li>
          <li><strong>Insurance and vetting.</strong> Ask for the certificate of insurance and whether cleaners are employees or subcontractors. A cheaper quote from an uninsured subcontractor puts the risk on you.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">Why Condition Matters More Than Square Footage</h2>
        <p>
          Square footage gives us a starting range, but condition decides the final number, and clients
          consistently underestimate how much. A 1,800 square foot home cleaned fortnightly takes roughly
          half the time of the same home left untouched for two years. The difference is not dust volume —
          it is that accumulated soil bonds to surfaces and needs dwell time, agitation and sometimes a
          second pass, none of which can be rushed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Four conditions change the estimate most in practice. Heavy kitchen grease on cabinet uppers and
          range hoods, which needs a dedicated degreaser and often a hand-polish afterwards. Discoloured
          grout in bathrooms and kitchens, which needs steam treatment rather than a mop. Pet hair woven
          into upholstery and carpet, which needs repeated vacuum passes with the right attachment. And
          hard water or soap scum on glass and fixtures, which needs a descaling acid that many surfaces
          cannot tolerate. Tell us about any of these when you request a quote and the number you receive
          will be accurate rather than optimistic.
        </p>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">Recurring Plans, Honestly Assessed</h2>
        <p>
          A recurring plan is the best value we offer, and it is also not right for everyone. It works
          because a maintained home never reaches the condition that makes a clean expensive — so we can
          charge less per visit and still do the job properly. Weekly, fortnightly and monthly plans all
          carry a per-visit discount against one-time pricing, and the discount deepens as frequency
          increases.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where a plan does not make sense is a property that is empty most of the year, a short-term
          rental between tenancies, or a home where the occupants are away for long stretches. In those
          cases an occasional deep clean every few months costs less over a year than a plan you are not
          present to benefit from. We will tell you that rather than sell you the subscription.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no contract on residential plans. You can pause or cancel at any time, and if you skip a
          month the price simply returns to the one-time rate for the next visit rather than resetting to a
          penalty. Commercial accounts run on a thirty-day notice period with pricing fixed for twelve
          months.
        </p>
        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">What Is Never an Extra Charge</h2>
        <p>
          A low headline price is often funded by charging separately for the things you assumed were
          included. These are the items other companies in this market bill as add-ons and that we treat as
          standard, so you can compare our number against theirs on equal terms.
        </p>
        <ul class="tick-list">
          <li><strong>All cleaning products and equipment.</strong> Commercial-grade vacuums, steamers, colour-coded microfiber and plant-derived solutions come with the crew at no additional cost.</li>
          <li><strong>Inside the oven and refrigerator.</strong> Included on every deep clean and on recurring plans by arrangement. Some operators charge these as two separate extras.</li>
          <li><strong>Interior windows, sills and tracks.</strong> Part of a standard clean, not an upsell.</li>
          <li><strong>Baseboards, door frames, switch plates and vents.</strong> Detailed on every visit rather than only on a quarterly upgrade.</li>
          <li><strong>Travel anywhere inside the service radius.</strong> No distance surcharge in Kansas or Missouri.</li>
          <li><strong>The re-clean if we miss something in the agreed scope.</strong> No charge, no argument, no third visit billed.</li>
          <li><strong>The written quote itself.</strong> Some companies charge a call-out fee for an estimate. Our walkthrough and quote are free and carry no obligation.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The genuine exclusions are equally straightforward: mould remediation and biohazard clean-up,
          exterior windows above ground level, heavy hoarding situations, and any task that needs
          scaffolding or specialist access equipment. Where something falls outside the scope we will say so
          during the quote rather than leaving it to be discovered on the day.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

